course: dictionary
lesson: 7974
title: Recognise and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Recognise has more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one to use. The first meaning is to know who or what someone is because you have seen, heard or met them before. «Each chick has its own voice, and its parents recognise it among thousands of others.» The parents know their own chick by its voice.
explain: The second meaning is to accept that something is true or real. The third meaning is to show that you value something someone has done, often by praising or rewarding it. «The school will recognise her good work with a prize.»
explain: To choose the right meaning, look at the clues around the word. If the sentence is about faces, voices or shapes, it means to know. If it is about a prize or praise, it means to reward. Then put your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Recognise means to know who or what something is because you have seen, heard or met it before.
fact: «Every gorilla has its own pattern of nose wrinkles, so scientists can recognise each one from photos.»
fact: «A study found sheep can recognise up to about 50 sheep faces and remember them for years.»
fact: «Coats of arms let knights be recognised when helmets hid their faces.»
fact: Recognise can also mean to accept that something is true, or to praise and reward someone's good work.
exercise: «Each chick has its own voice, and its parents recognise it among thousands of others.» What does recognise mean here? | *To know who it is | To run away from | To cook a meal for | To lose track of
exercise: «The school will recognise her good work with a prize.» What does recognise mean here? | To copy it | To ignore it | *To praise and reward it | To forget it
exercise: Which word means about the same as recognise when you know someone from before? | Hide | *Identify | Forget | Miss
test: Coats of arms let knights be recognised when helmets hid their faces. What does recognised mean? | Sold | Hidden | Lost | *Known for who they are
test: The leader said he would recognise the mistake. What does recognise mean here? | Forget it | Laugh at it | Hide it | *Accept that it is true
exam: Jayden reads «She could recognise his laugh from the next room.» What does recognise mean here? | To lose his laugh | To laugh too loudly | To tell a joke | *To know him from his laugh
